"Usage" in this codebase is the cobra.Command.Usage method, defined at command.go:446 (top-ranked seed, score 140). Its snippet (evidence e37, command.go:440-452) is:

// Usage puts out the usage for the command.
// Used when a user provides invalid input.
// Can be defined by user by overriding UsageFunc.
func (c *Command) Usage() error {
	return c.UsageFunc()(c)
}

So Usage() prints the usage message for a command, and is triggered when a user supplies invalid input. It delegates to c.UsageFunc()(c) rather than doing formatting itself.

UsageFunc (command.go:426, evidence e36, snippet lines 420-432) supplies that function:
// UsageFunc returns either the function set by SetUsageFunc for this command
// or a parent, or it returns a default usage function.
func (c *Command) UsageFunc() (f func(*Command) error) {
	if c.usageFunc != nil {
		return c.usageFunc
	}
	if c.HasParent() {
		return c.Parent().UsageFunc()
	}
	...

This shows usage behavior is overridable per-command (via c.usageFunc) and falls back through the parent command chain (c.HasParent() / c.Parent().UsageFunc()), otherwise using a built-in default.

The function can be set explicitly via SetUsageFunc (command.go:307, evidence e33, snippet 301-313):
// SetUsageFunc sets usage function. Usage can be defined by application.
func (c *Command) SetUsageFunc(f func(*Command) error) {
	c.usageFunc = f
}

There is also SetUsageTemplate (command.go, same snippet block) for setting a template string, and a corresponding UsageTemplate() getter (command.go:544, evidence e40, snippet 538-550):
// UsageTemplate returns usage template for the command.
func (c *Command) UsageTemplate() string {
	if c.usageTemplate != "" {
		return c.usageTemplate
	}
	if c.HasParent() {
		return c.parent.UsageTemplate()
	}
	...

Related supporting pieces surfaced by the graph:
- UsageString (command.go:479) — related/caller of Usage, presumably renders the usage text as a string.
- UsagePadding (command.go:516, evidence e35, snippet 510-522) — computes left-padding for usage listings, using a package-level minUsagePadding = 25, falling back to c.parent.commandsMaxUseLen if larger.
- PrintErrln (command.go:1416, callee) and OutOrStderr (command.go:380, callee) — used in the usage-printing/error-output path.
- HasParent (command.go:1632) and mergePersistentFlags (command.go:1849) — additional callees tied into command-tree traversal used by the usage machinery.
- The Command struct itself (command.go:51) is the receiver type for all of the above.

Documentation: site/content/user_guide.md:583 has a "Defining your own usage" section (evidence e34, snippet lines 577-589):
### Defining your own usage
You can provide your own usage function or template for Cobra to use.
Like help, the function and template are overridable through public methods:

```go
cmd.SetUsageFunc(f func(*Command) error)
cmd.SetUsageTemplate(s string)

This confirms the public customization API is exactly SetUsageFunc and SetUsageTemplate, matching the two setter methods found in command.go.

In short: "Usage" is the mechanism (Command.Usage/UsageFunc/UsageTemplate/UsageString/UsagePadding, all in command.go) that cobra uses to print/format a command's usage message on invalid input, customizable per-command via SetUsageFunc/SetUsageTemplate, inheriting from parent commands when not explicitly set, and documented in site/content/user_guide.md under "Defining your own usage."
